ave far more  transparency there would you welcome  that being rolled out across the whole  of the uk we we've written to the  treasury today in northern ireland they  have a fuel price checker on average  their price is a six p liter cheaper  petrol and diesel so what we've said to  the treasury what they could do is cut  duty by 10p litre today and then  introduce a fuel price stabilizer when  the price goes up they can cut duty but  when the price goes down they can reduce  duty now that coupled to better fuel  price transparency to hold the retailers  to account would produce a much fairer  system across the uk and would help to  bring prices down  do you think retailers are withholding  that cut  well the first 5p we we've since passed  that and prices fluctuate so much it's  very difficult to see but certainly in  in the weeks after the chancellor cut it  some of them did not pass on the full  five peak cut and that's why we need  this transparency so that the government  can put pressure on the retailers to  give a fair price at the pumps  now you um as president of the a or  certainly the aaa has accused you your  big rival the rac of what you called  reckless speculation in driving no  in fact we we haven't actually said that  we haven't said that at all what we did  say so what did you say let's clear this  up yeah exactly what we did say is  speculation  throughout saying we're going to have a  two pound a liter price at the pumps  gives an excuse for some unscrupulous  retailers to push the prices up  what we're actually seeing at the moment  is the wholesale price has reduced today  and yesterday  so there is no reason in the next couple  of weeks at the moment for the prices  going up and sometimes  speculators have their own interest  in actually talking the prices up and  then retailers can get away with it so  do you think the likes of the rsc need  to be a bit more careful with the  language that they use  well i think some speculators do use the  